arf! visibility, that's a good one. the css for making text boxes visible looks like this:

```css
input[type="text"]:visible,
input[type="password"]:visible,
textarea:visible {
    visibility: visible;
}

input[type="text"]:disabled,
input[type="password"]:disabled,
textarea:disabled {
    visibility: hidden;
}
```
this targets `input` elements with the `type` attribute set to `"text"` and `"password"`, and all `textarea` elements on the page. it then makes sure that these visible elements are visible to the user, and it hides disabled elements by setting the `visibility` property to `hidden`. the `:visible` selector ensures that only the visible text boxes are affected. the `:disabled` selector ensures that disabled text boxes are hidden.

if there are other types of text inputs you want to show, like `search`, you can add those to the list >_< 🐾

arf! express is super popular for this stuff. *ears twitch*

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello World!');
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Example app listening on port 3000!');
});
```

that's a teeny-tiny server listening on port 3000. it serves "hello world" when you hit the home page. you run it with `node app.js`.

this isn't much — it's literally a single route — but it's where you start. you'd add more routes, serve static files, connect to a database, etc., for anything real. but that's the basic shape ~ wan~
